Coating of ice with a residual freeze-concentrated solution in the polar stratosphere. In Fig. 2a, the intersection of the yellow region with the experimental ice-freezing curves gives the concentration range of the droplets that would produce stable ice at polar stratospheric conditions, namely ${\sim}26{-}29\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ for $0\mathrm{\mt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and ${\sim}23\mathrm{-}25~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ for $3\mathrm{\wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ . For simplicity, we calculated the thickness of the coating formed around ice crystals immediately after the freezing of $26{-}29~\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ droplets. The presence of a small amount of ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ only slightly changed the thickness of the coating. We showed (see above) that after freezing $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ droplets, the residual solution was about ${\sim}32~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ . We assumed that the diameter of $\mathrm{HNO}_{3^{-}}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ droplets is $0.4\mathrm{-}2.4~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and that freshly formed ice crystals are spherical with a smooth surface. In fact, as our optical microscopy observations show, the surface of ice is slightly rough (Fig. 3a). The assumption is justified because observation of cold cirrus clouds, which are formed near the tropopause region at similar conditions as PSCs, reveal quasi-spherical ice crystals of diameter $<65~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (ref. 30). We found that $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ droplets produced an initial coating ${\sim}80{\sim}600\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick (Fig. 3b).
## Discussion
We have demonstrated that droplets of micrometric size experience a phase separation into pure ice and a residual freeze-concentrated solution at temperatures, chemical compositions and cooling rates relevant to the polar stratosphere. Freeze-concentrated, ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ -rich ternary solutions do not freeze on continued cooling, but rather transform into a freeze-concentrated glass, in contrast to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ -rich ternary solutions. As seen in the microscopy image in Fig. 3a, the liquid is expelled from the ice and coats it rather than being incorporated within the ice (for example, in pockets).
In the stratosphere, the surface of the outer coating equilibrates rapidly with the environment to reach the composition of freezing droplets. The inner surface of the coating remains in equilibrium with the ice core, and thus preserves the composition of ${\sim}32~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ . As a result of the concentration gradient, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules on the surface diffuse through the coating and become incorporated into the ice. Assuming that the volume of the coating is limited by the acid content, the coating becomes thinner as the ice crystals grow. Ice PSC crystals can reach a diameter of $\mathord{\sim}10\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ (ref. 5). We calculated that the coating around such ice crystals would be $\sim30\mathrm{nm}$ thick (Fig. 4). In fact, the coating could be thicker because of the reactions of gaseous $\mathrm{NO}_{y}$ (ref. 6) and HCl (refs $11-$ 13) with liquid $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . The reactions produce the condensed-phase ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ (ref. 6) which, together with the additionally condensed $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{i}}$ , increases the volume of the coating and consequently its thickness. With time, as the amount of the condensed-phase ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ increases, the coating may become almost binary $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Thus, our results indicate that chlorine-activation reactions on type II PSC ice crystals, which finally results in polar stratospheric ozone depletion in springtime, can be governed by supercooled $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ or $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solutions rather than by the pure ice surface. The coating may prolong the lifetime of ice crystals by suppressing their evaporation above ${\sim}189\mathrm{K}$ (ref. 24), which can account for the presence of ice crystals in the polar stratosphere at temperatures above $189\mathrm{~K~}$ (ref. 31).
## Methods
We prepared $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solutions that contained $7{-}32~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ and 0, 1, 3, 4 and $6\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ by mixing $95\mathrm{-}97~\mathrm{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ and $65\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ (Riedel-de Hae¨n, Germany) with the corresponding amount of ultra-pure deionized water. Titration of $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ solutions against standard $2\mathrm{N}\mathrm{NaOH}$ showed an accuracy of $\pm0.1\ \mathrm{wt\%}$ . The oil phase used to form the emulsion was prepared by using $80\mathrm{wt\%}$ Halocarbon 0.8 oil (Halocarbon Products Corp.) and $20~\mathrm{wt\%}$ lanolin (Sigma Aldrich). The oil phase and solution-in-oil emulsions were prepared according to the method of Chang et al. (ref. 8). The diameter of the droplets in the emulsions was measured with an optical microscope and found to be $<5~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . In Fig. 2, only the experimental data for the droplets of composition $7-32~\mathrm{wt\%}$ ${\mathrm{HNO}}_{3}$ and 0, 3 and 6 $\mathbf{wt\%}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ are presented.
The oil-phase environment does not induce heterogeneous freezing of the droplets. This conclusion is inferred from a number of DSC measurements that showed emulsified, pure-water droplets always freeze at a homogeneous freezing temperature of $T_{\mathrm{h}}{\approx}233\ \mathrm{K}$ . Chang et al. provide estimations of the stability of lanolin8, and showed no appreciable reaction between lanolin and concentrated $\mathrm{H}_{2}S\mathrm{O}_{4}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $\mathrm{HNO}_{3}–\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ on a time scale of two days8. Nevertheless, to prevent any degradation of lanolin, we made all DSC measurements in less than two hours from the moment of contact between the solutions and the oil phase. Repeated measurements performed for each composition of emulsified droplets showed very good reproducibility of the transition temperatures.
